{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>When you come, etc. and I will give, etc.</b> Now is this good tidings? He should have said only ״כִּי יִהְיֶה נֶגַע בְּקִירוֹת בֵּית אֲחֻזַּתְכֶם״ etc., in the manner he mentioned concerning the plagues of human beings. And our Rabbis of blessed memory were moved by this and said (Leviticus Rabbah ch. 17) that when the Canaanites heard that Israel was coming to their land, they hid away everything, etc. — thus far. And this is the way of derash. And based on what they said in the Midrash (there), and this is their language: Rabbi Levi said, the Master of mercy does not strike at persons first, etc. — at first the plagues come upon his house, etc. — thus far — it will be explained correctly. And it is because He prefaced by stating the plagues of human beings, which is the opposite of what is said of the Master of mercy; therefore He said: what I prefaced — the plagues of human beings — is because you have not yet come to the land and there are no houses to be stricken; but ״כִּי תָבֹאוּ אֶל הָאָרֶץ״ — when you come to the land — then I will not give the plague upon the person first, but rather ״וְנָתַתִּי נֶגַע בְּבֵית אֶרֶץ״ etc. first." "And the reason the verse did not arrange the plague of the house first is because plagues of a person exist immediately, from the giving of the Torah onward, whereas plagues of the house apply only when they come to the land; therefore He placed first what is needed immediately. And if you say: if so, He should have placed plagues of garments before plagues of a person, since both of them exist immediately, and for them it would be fitting to precede according to the order of the attribute of the Master of mercy. And it appears that HaShem chose to arrange plagues of garments between plagues of a person and plagues of houses, because there are matters in them that are equal to plagues of a person and matters that are equal to plagues of houses — for in the measurement of the צרעת they are equal to the law of plagues of a person, by a gris, and not like the צרעת of houses, which is two grisin. And in the colors they are equal to plagues of houses — יְרַקְרַק or אֲדַמְדָּם — and not in the color white like the plague of a person." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that one should note why the verse arranged the laws of the plague of the garment before it completed all the laws of the plagues of human beings — their impurity and their purification — for it should have waited until it completed the entire judgment of a person's צרעת and only then arranged the judgment of garments. And one cannot say that the verse intended to arrange the laws of impurity by themselves and the laws of their purification by themselves, for if so, why did it not do likewise with the צרעת of the house, where it should also have preceded the impurity of the plague of the house before the judgment of the purification of the plagues of a person and of his garments? Rather, it is certain that the verse showed in this that it preceded the plague of the garment before the plague of a person and before His saying ״זֹאת תִּהְיֶה תּוֹרַת הַמְּצֹרָע״ — in which He hinted at the sign of the sin of צרעת, that his mouth testified against it, as we explained at the beginning of the parashah — [and therefore] He wrote plague of garments, and His intention is to say that the garments are stricken first. And as for why He began with the law of the bringing of the plague upon a person and did not begin with garments, in this the verse was wise to strengthen the matter that plagues of garments also come because of the sin of the person like plagues of a person; therefore He began with plagues of a person and adjacent to them He stated plagues of garments, and after both of them He said ״זֹאת תִּהְיֶה תּוֹרַת הַמְּצֹרָע״ — the law of him who brings forth an evil name. You can see to know that these matters apply also to plagues of garments, which would not have been understood thus if He had arranged [only] plagues of garments. And understand." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that the verse was careful to precede the plague of the garment and afterward plagues of a person, for otherwise I would say that the plague of the garment comes for a light sin and the plague of a person comes for a severe sin, and not for his own sin that he should be liable to have a plague come upon the person — rather it comes upon the garments because of mercy; therefore the verse preceded and stated plagues of a person, to say that even the צרעת that needed to come upon the person — the Master of mercy brings it upon the garments, or perhaps He will again heal him so that it need not come upon him. And yet I still say: perhaps for a severe sin plagues of a person come, and for a light sin plagues of garments come, and the Torah arranged the severe first — which are plagues of a person — and afterward the light — which are plagues of garments. If so, He should have arranged the judgment of garments after the judgment of a person and their purification, as I wrote above. And understand."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:09.313275374Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}